{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 9</p>
  <h1>Ship</h1>
  <p class="lede">One file. No runtime to install, no proxy in front, no container.</p>
</header>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <pre class="term"><code>./roux build main.roc
ls -lh main
ROUX_PORT=8081 ./main</code></pre>
  <p>That is the whole app, optimized: the server, your Roc, the templates' bytecode,
  SQLite. Open <a href="http://localhost:8081">localhost:8081</a> beside this one.
  <code>static/</code> is read from beside it, and <code>tutor.db</code> is made there.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>On a server</h2>
  <p>Copy <code>main</code> and <code>static/</code> to any x86-64 Linux machine and run it. For
  HTTPS it gets its own certificate from Let's Encrypt at start, and redirects plain HTTP:</p>
  <pre class="term"><code>ROUX_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0 ROUX_PORT=443 ROUX_REDIRECT_PORT=80 \
ROUX_ACME_DIRECTORY=https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory \
ROUX_ACME_IDENTIFIER=example.com ROUX_ACME_STATE=/var/lib/myapp \
./main</code></pre>
  <p>The server is <a href="https://github.com/Eldhus/fourneau">fourneau</a>, written in Zig
  for exactly this: io_uring, a thread per core, your handlers on fibers. The race it runs
  every night against Go, Rust and others is itself a roux app:
  <a href="https://fourneau.y2kbugger.com/">fourneau.y2kbugger.com</a>.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Where next</h2>
